Demonstration of proper Symbol Table Construction \
\
The following query shows all of the typical places where a select subquery might be included in a properly constructed SQL statement. The most typical case is the insertion within a from-join clause. This query shows two additional scenarios:  subqueries producing scalar values in place of a predicand, and as a list of values to control an IN list. \
\
select tab1.a aa, \
       (select max(D) from ee) max_D, \
       (select min(D) from ee) min_D,  \
       kk.w\
 from tab1\cf4 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb3  join (select w from jj) kk\cf4 \cb1 \
\cf2 \cb3  where a in (select c from ff)\
\
\cf4 \cb1 \
\
These two additional scenarios require the definition of additional subordinate symbol tables, one for each of these special uses. The contents of these subordinate contexts do not bleed out into the symbol table context of the main query (although they could reference symbols from the context they\'92ve been injected into, the surrounding context cannot access any symbol within their context, except as emitted by their interface.\
\
This is reflected by the fact that the references to the lookup function points to the unique query<x> reference assigned by the parser, but their actual self contained symbol tables fall under the \'93def_query<x>\'94 labels. \
\
The more typical symbol table of the outermost query shows that the column references available in the query are defined by tab1 and \'93query2\'94 and that all directly accessed column symbols are accounted for by the entries in the from-join clause.\
\
