\f0 \cf2 \cb3 Possible Clauses in the Symbol Table and What They Mean \
\
Every symbol table represents the scoping context of a single SQL level. It contains the simplified relationships between the data symbols input into the context from external tables or from embedded subqueries, the complete list of column references fully-defined by their origins and related to the output interface of the context. Every scoping context in a SQL statement represents one enclosed naming space for the query execution process, not unlike the program stack representing a function call in other languages.\
\
SQL presents a multi-dimensional functional output consisting of one or more output columns defined by the context\'92s \'93interface\'94 from which might flow zero or more rows (tuples) of data related through the SQL logic and data structures themselves. Hence, every symbol table defines a single \'93interface\'94 entry, listing the output column names of the defined context.\
\
In the symbol table, the \'93interface\'94 key contains a map consisting of the output column label, which is either an explicit alias assigned to a column, the column\'92s actual name, or is derived based on other information (NOTE: There is no standard, universal way that columns are defined across different database engines. In Panto the symbol table will depict this with a generic column named prefix \'93unnamed_\'94 followed by an arbitrary number to make them unique. )\
\
Interface:\{A:col1, B:value1, C:unnamed_0\}\
\
\
The typical symbol table will consist of paired alias-table or alias-subquery or alias-values statements if any aliases are indicated in the from and join statements. These aliases would be the ones used as prefixes for columns and will indicate which of the source tuples (e.g., table, subquery or values statement) where a given column reference originates. An example entry for each might look the following examples:\
\
A:phys_tab_name\
B:query0\
C:values3\
\
If the Sql statement references a table name directly, it might not have an alias, but there should always be an alias entry for a query or values statement. For any subquery used in the from or join statement, the prefix \'93query\'94 followed by a number is most often used. However. query references can have other prefixes, including \'93union\'94 and \'93intersect\'94. Values statements when referenced in the symbol table will have the prefix \'93values\'94 and a number. \
\
Each of these references in a symbol table represent primary inputs to the symbol context. Think of these entries as the list of symbols that are contributed to the query level context represented by the symbol table. Hence, looking at a symbol table like:\
\
Tab1:\{a:\'85, b:\'85\}\
\
Can be read as:\
\
The table TAB1 has contributed column symbols \'93a\'94 and \'93b\'94 to the context.\
\
The primary property of these references is that they define through their own interfaces (or table dictionaries for external tables) the only column references permitted within the context (with the exception of inherited references for correlated subqueries, which will be explained below).\
\
If a query contains references to any column names that are not defined by the interfaces of the embedded subqueries or values statements in the from-join clauses, then it is assumed they must be defined within the external table references in the from-join clause. If there are any column references not associated to a known local interface or eventually to a table dictionary reference for included tables, then those references indicate a run-time error. The parser alone cannot identify these possible run time errors, so a validation cycle has been added during construction of the parser symbol table to register any unaccounted for column references and add error and warning messages to the parse results.}